A||||4||JohnnyK||Any way to check clutch wear?||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||05-10-2000||04:59 PM||||Is there a fairly easy way to check how much life left on the clutch (since I know nothing about this cars history)... It has 138 miles on it.. What are the chances that it has already been replaced and won't need it for a while? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Tom88gt||05-10-2000||05:15 PM||||WOW!!!  A Fiero with only 138 miles!!!  I'd say your clutch should last about 10,000 years at the current usage rate.<P>If you meant to say you had 138,000 miles on the car...then you are due for a new clutch, if it has never been replaced before.  You can't check it without removing it.  When it first begins to slip, stop and replace ASAP.  This is an item you can't put off, only plan for.  If you replace as soon as it first starts to slip, you can save the pressure plate and only have to replace the disc.  You can reuse the plate and bearing if they are still good with no signs of wear.  However, it is such a time consuming process to replace the disc, and the plate, disc and bearing can be purchased as set for a few $ more than just the disc, only a fool would not replace all at the same time.  It's not worth saving a few bucks if you have to replace the other 2 parts in a year or two.||||reg||10
Z||000002||Homicidal||05-11-2000||01:47 AM||||All depends on use.  Mine only has 67,000 and I needed a new clutch.  Check the master + slave cylinder, also check the pushrod and clutch release lever.<P>Those are cheap parts that can cause BIG problems!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ed||05-12-2000||12:56 AM||||Do you have any obvious problems?  If not, don't worry, be happy. <P>You can try to slip it, to see if it has some weakness in it, but why try?  If it isn't slipping, or sticky, you don't need to fool with it anyway.  <P>Confirming what Tom said, there is no inspection access, so no direct observation of the disc is possible.<P>I replaced mine at 110,000 (V-6), but that was because the rear cam seal leaked oil onto the disc. The disc itself still had an adequate friction surface. <P>P.S. for Cliff. Obviously, I've got access again. Thanks.    <p>[This message has been edited by Ed (edited 05-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnnyK||05-12-2000||09:49 AM||||Nope, if I let out the clutch about a few inches, the rpm's drop dramatically.. I hope to god that the owner replaced it, and THEN let it sit outside for years..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
